CASE = [
 Q("<b>Case study.</b> Gurpreet, a farmer in Sangrur, Punjab, has to bore his tubewell deeper every few years. Most farmers nearby grow wheat and paddy, which need a lot of water, and cheap electricity encourages heavy pumping. An officer tells the village that about 80 per cent of Punjab's area is classed as over-exploited for groundwater. (i) Which resource is being depleted in the passage? (ii) Give one reason for its decline. (iii) What term describes using a resource faster than Nature can renew it? (iv) Suggest two measures that can help and explain how they work.",
   "(i) Groundwater (1). (ii) Water-intensive crops (wheat, paddy) and heavy pumping (1). (iii) Over-exploitation (1). (iv) Crop diversification reduces demand for water, and rain-water harvesting recharges the aquifer (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q("<b>Case study.</b> Pema's farm in Sikkim had tired soil. She stopped using chemicals and began using compost, neem sprays and mixed cropping. Yields dipped for two years and then rose. Sikkim became India's first fully organic state in 2016, and visitors now come to enjoy its clean produce and landscapes. (i) Which state became India's first fully organic state, and in which year? (ii) Name two natural inputs used by Pema. (iii) What word means using resources wisely with care so that renewables can regenerate? (iv) State two benefits of the shift to organic farming.",
   "(i) Sikkim, 2016 (1). (ii) Any two: compost, neem, mixed cropping (1). (iii) Stewardship (1). (iv) Soil and water are protected from chemical build-up, and organic produce earns higher prices and attracts tourism (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
 Q("<b>Case study.</b> A company finds iron ore in a hill near a tribal village. The ore lies very deep, and the cost of extracting it is higher than its market price. The villagers also regard the hill as sacred. (i) Name the three conditions that make something a resource. (ii) Which condition fails because of the high cost? (iii) Which condition fails because of the villagers' belief? (iv) Why can the same material be a resource in one period and not in another? Give one example.",
   "(i) Technologically accessible, economically feasible and culturally acceptable (1). (ii) Economic feasibility (1). (iii) Cultural acceptability (1). (iv) Value depends on technology, economy and culture, which change over time; for example oil was of little use before engines were invented (2).", "1+1+1+2"),
]

